#% # The root budgets every repository create-repo composes starts with, rendered by
#% # `compose_repo_plan.py --ci-cd REPO_DIR` (see ci.yml.template for the `#%` syntax).
# The budgets this repository holds itself to, checked by `onebudgetspec check budgets.yaml`.
#
# Nine are its CI and CD figures, labelled `release-tail`: measured after a release has
# published, by scripts/actions-budget-figures.sh (a verbatim copy of the create-repo skill's)
# from read-only GitHub evidence (onevcs for the existing figures, the cache script for
# replay telemetry), as the last step of release.yml's `publish` job, whose result nothing
# reads. The tenth, `host-variable`, is the local gate's wall clock, which
# `just gate` checks as a warning. The strict check `just check` runs excludes both labels, and
# ci.yml's `budgets` job does not enforce them. It reports the two CI-time figures from its own
# run and the cache headroom, and omits the rest, labelled `not-measured-on-pr`: a pull request
# cannot measure those or move their figures, and their check elsewhere is unchanged. Its push
# run at the release commit delivers nothing, so the CD figures leave out the `ci`
# workflow@@CD_EXCLUDED_PROSE@@.
# references/ci.md ("CI and CD time budgets") states why each line is where it is.
schema_version: 1
budgets:
  # llmlint: ignore[budgets_track_product_owner_outcomes] The brief requires a 30-minute line per CI check and per CD job beside the 90-minute release cycle that contains them, job counts per PR push and per release, and GitHub reads per release and per pull request; the skill ships these constraints plus the requested shared-cache replay figure.
  - id: @@NAME@@-ci-check-time
    measure: reported
    command: [bash, scripts/actions-budget-figures.sh, longest-job, --stage, pr]
    unit: seconds
    direction: max
    threshold: 1800
    labels: [release-tail]
    description: >-
      The longest counted job of each pull request's last-commit CI runs, over the pull
      requests one release carries, from GitHub's job timestamps. It protects every
      contributor's turnaround, because one slow check holds every change behind it;
      ci.yml's `timeout-minutes: 30` enforces the same line on each pull request.
  # llmlint: ignore[budgets_track_product_owner_outcomes] The brief requires a 30-minute line per CI check and per CD job beside the 90-minute release cycle that contains them, job counts per PR push and per release, and GitHub reads per release and per pull request; the skill ships these constraints plus the requested shared-cache replay figure.
  - id: @@NAME@@-cd-job-time
    measure: reported
    command: [bash, scripts/actions-budget-figures.sh, longest-job, --stage, cd, --exclude-workflow, @@CD_EXCLUDED_WORKFLOWS@@]
    unit: seconds
    direction: max
    threshold: 1800
    labels: [release-tail, not-measured-on-pr]
    description: >-
      The longest counted job of one release's CD runs — release-plz's run at the release
      commit and the Release run, publish included up to the tail's moment. It protects
      the time a release takes, a slow job being the actionable unit inside the release
      cycle.
  # llmlint: ignore[budgets_track_product_owner_outcomes] The brief requires a 30-minute line per CI check and per CD job beside the 90-minute release cycle that contains them, job counts per PR push and per release, and GitHub reads per release and per pull request; the skill ships these constraints plus the requested shared-cache replay figure.
  - id: @@NAME@@-release-cycle-time
    measure: reported
    command: [bash, scripts/actions-budget-figures.sh, release-cycle-time, --published-paths, .github/release-published-paths]
    unit: seconds
    direction: max
    threshold: 5400
    labels: [release-tail, not-measured-on-pr]
    description: >-
      Per pull request whose merge changes a published artifact path, its last commit's
      checks plus its merge to the end of publish; the maximum over those requests protects how soon a
      merged change reaches the people who install it.
  # llmlint: ignore[budgets_track_product_owner_outcomes] The brief requires a 30-minute line per CI check and per CD job beside the 90-minute release cycle that contains them, job counts per PR push and per release, and GitHub reads per release and per pull request; the skill ships these constraints plus the requested shared-cache replay figure.
  - id: @@NAME@@-ci-job-count
    measure: reported
    command: [bash, scripts/actions-budget-figures.sh, job-count, --stage, pr]
    unit: jobs
    direction: max
    threshold: @@CI_JOB_COUNT@@
    labels: [release-tail]
    description: >-
      The most counted jobs any pull request of one release ran at its last commit, over
      every workflow its pull_request event started. It protects this repository's share
      of the account-wide 20-job concurrency limit: @@CI_JOBS_PROSE@@.
  # llmlint: ignore[budgets_track_product_owner_outcomes] The brief requires a 30-minute line per CI check and per CD job beside the 90-minute release cycle that contains them, job counts per PR push and per release, and GitHub reads per release and per pull request; the skill ships these constraints plus the requested shared-cache replay figure.
  - id: @@NAME@@-cd-job-count
    measure: reported
    command: [bash, scripts/actions-budget-figures.sh, job-count, --stage, cd, --exclude-workflow, @@CD_EXCLUDED_WORKFLOWS@@]
    unit: jobs
    direction: max
    threshold: @@CD_JOB_COUNT@@
    labels: [release-tail, not-measured-on-pr]
    description: >-
      The counted jobs of one release's CD runs, publish included as the job running the
      tail. It protects this repository's CD share of the account-wide 20-job concurrency
      limit: @@CD_JOBS_PROSE@@.
  # llmlint: ignore[budgets_track_product_owner_outcomes] The brief requires a 30-minute line per CI check and per CD job beside the 90-minute release cycle that contains them, job counts per PR push and per release, and GitHub reads per release and per pull request; the skill ships these constraints plus the requested shared-cache replay figure.
  - id: @@NAME@@-release-read-requests
    measure: reported
    command: [bash, scripts/actions-budget-figures.sh, release-read-requests, --exclude-workflow, @@CD_EXCLUDED_WORKFLOWS@@]
    unit: requests
    direction: max
    threshold: 6
    labels: [release-tail, not-measured-on-pr]
    description: >-
      The REST requests the release's own reads cost — the releases listing, the
      comparison, the closed-pull-request page, the runs at the release commit and the
      jobs of its CD runs — summed from onevcs's answers. It protects the tail's GitHub
      reads from growing with this repository's CD shape, whatever number of pull requests
      a release carries.
  # llmlint: ignore[budgets_track_product_owner_outcomes] The brief requires a 30-minute line per CI check and per CD job beside the 90-minute release cycle that contains them, job counts per PR push and per release, and GitHub reads per release and per pull request; the skill ships these constraints plus the requested shared-cache replay figure.
  - id: @@NAME@@-pr-read-requests
    measure: reported
    command: [bash, scripts/actions-budget-figures.sh, pr-read-requests]
    unit: requests
    direction: max
    threshold: 2
    labels: [release-tail, not-measured-on-pr]
    description: >-
      The most REST requests any one pull request of the release needed — its runs at head
      and the jobs of each CI run there — from onevcs's answers. It protects the reads
      each pull request costs from growing with the workflows a pull request starts.
  # llmlint: ignore[budgets_track_product_owner_outcomes] The brief requires a 30-minute line per CI check and per CD job beside the 90-minute release cycle that contains them, job counts per PR push and per release, and GitHub reads per release and per pull request; the skill ships these constraints plus the requested shared-cache replay figure.
  - id: @@NAME@@-gate-time
    measure: reported
    command: [node, scripts/gate-time-budget.mjs]
    unit: seconds
    direction: max
    threshold: 1800
    labels: [host-variable, not-measured-on-pr]
    description: >-
      The wall clock of one `just gate` run on a development machine, from the start and
      end the recipe records on that run. It holds the local gate to the line each CI job
      is held to, as a warning, since a development machine's timing varies too much to
      refuse on.

  - id: @@NAME@@-nx-cache-use
    measure: reported
    command: [bash, scripts/actions-budget-figures.sh, cache-use]
    unit: percent
    direction: min
    threshold: 100
    labels: [release-tail, not-measured-on-pr]
    description: >-
      Remote replay of previously attempted reusable task results, by exact task, OS
      and hash, on the release pull request and same-PR retries or later pushes.
      Detects reusable results that become unavailable before a later run needs them.

  # The threshold is onetaskgraph's measured figure, until this repository's warm runs have
  # saved: replace it then by one whole warm run's saves plus each family's largest growth.
  - id: @@NAME@@-cache-headroom
    measure: reported
    command: [bash, scripts/actions-budget-figures.sh, cache-headroom@@CACHE_HEADROOM_FAMILIES@@]
    unit: gigabytes
    direction: min
    threshold: 3.05
    labels: [release-tail]
    description: >-
      The room left under GitHub's Actions cache line, in gigabytes, counting each named
      family's newest copy and every other entry in full. GitHub removes the least recently
      read entries once a save finds the listing over the line, so it protects the proven
      test results a release reuses from being removed by this repository's own builds.
